WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.560
ROGER MARTINEZ:
Today, we're talking

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:05.060
about Cloud Run, Cloud Logging,
and best practices for Compute

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:05.970
Engine.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.580
I'm Roger Martinez, and welcome
to "This Week in Cloud,"

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.170
where we serve you
the lowest latency

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.810
news from the safety of home.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.960
Cloud Run is now
integrated with Cloud Code,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.090
making it easier to
develop with Cloud Run

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.820
by incorporating common
workflows with your IDE,

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.160
like creating new
Cloud Run services.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.780
You can continue to
develop and debug

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.490
these services on
your local machine,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.710
then deploy them with Cloud Run.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.480
After that, once
they're running,

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.470
Cloud Code also helps you manage
and update those services.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.150
Cloud Logging was
designed to help

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:39.240
developers and operators
easily identify the cause

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.890
infrastructure issues.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.590
Since easy can always
be easier, we'll

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.720
keep working to
improve this service.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.507
For example, we just launched
the beta for suggested queries,

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:51.090
which will recommend
logs that provide

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.580
context on your recent
error reporting groups,

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.180
learning incidents, and
other valuable logging data.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.510
It's important to keep your
VMs up to date with OS patches,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.790
but managing that
for large deployments

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.220
can quickly become a challenge.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.110
While there isn't a magical
answer that works for everyone,

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.430
there are a number of best
practices that you can follow.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.340
This list was based off of
patterns of other Google Cloud

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.070
customers with large
Compute Engine deployments

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.410
and includes ideas like
maintaining flexible deployment

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.940
groups and setting
a disruption budget.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.890
The latest Google
Cloud whitepaper

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.540
is Cloud Key
Management Service Deep

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.780
Dive, which focuses on the inner
workings of Google's cloud key

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.600
management service platform and
the key management capabilities

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.448
that are currently available.

00:01:34.448 --> 00:01:35.990
That's because we
believe that making

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:38.420
encryption key management
more performant, secure,

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.490
and compliant will
only be possible

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.207
if it's done transparently.

00:01:43.207 --> 00:01:45.290
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.090
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.590
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.570
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.010
We'll see you in the Cloud.

